hat's true. You can keep the good and gently set aside what doesn't hold up. That's not betrayal; that's maturity. **3. The scariness fades, and what replaces it can be empowering.** Right now, it feels destabilizing. But on the other side of this is something valuable: the realization that you're not bound by every belief you inherited. You get to think for yourself. You get to update. And in this particular case, that update could literally change the course of your cousin's life. The fear you feel right now is the price of admission for that kind of impact. **4. You're not alone in this.** Countless people have gone through exactly what you're experiencing—having a foundational belief challenged by new information, feeling unmoored, and slowly rebuilding a more accurate picture of the world. It's a lonely feeling, but you're in a huge, invisible company of humans who've walked this path before you. And here's what I want to emphasize most: you don't have to figure all of this out today. You don't need to audit your entire family's belief system right now. The only thing in front of you is a girl who thinks she's stupid at math, and a cousin who cares enough to help. That's a small, manageable, beautiful thing. You can start there, and let the big questions take the time they need. The fact that you're willing to sit with this discomfort rather than just shutting down the conversation tells me something important about you. It tells me you're the kind of person who values truth, even when it's hard. And that's exactly the kind of person your cousin needs in her corner.
